  <h1>First steps into NYC's best-paid public jobs</h1>
  <p class="lede">Each card is <span class="ref" data-ref="start" tabindex="0" aria-describedby="ref-help">one way to start</span> a career with the City of New York: a trade, a civil-service exam, a degree, an appointment or an election. Together the 84 cards cover all 443 job titles that paid someone at least $200,000 in the <a href="https://data.cityofnewyork.us/d/k397-673e" target="_blank" rel="noopener">city's official 2025 payroll</a>. Each card shows the <span class="ref" data-ref="title" tabindex="0" aria-describedby="ref-help">best-paid job</span> its start led to, that job's <span class="ref" data-ref="pay" tabindex="0" aria-describedby="ref-help">2025 pay</span>, and the <span class="ref" data-ref="sources" tabindex="0" aria-describedby="ref-help">postings that set out the requirements</span>.</p>
  <p class="lede">That pay is the most any one person in the job received, not what the job typically pays. On more than half the cards it is over double the base salary, inflated by overtime or by one-off lump sums such as retroactive raises.</p>
  <span id="ref-help" class="sr-only">Highlights the matching part of the first card.</span>
</header>